  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nCurrent-Monday...Continued warm and humid early this morning with \ntemps and dewpoints well into the 70s. Surface high pressure ridging \nwill continue to slowly slip south/east away from ECFL with the \napproach of a surface boundary across the Deep South. This weak \nfront will move into north FL by early Mon morning with perhaps a \nbit more southward advancement into north/central Fl by the end of \nthe period. Light SW/W winds will transition onshore in the \nafternoon along the coast with ECSB formation (possibly delayed)\nand slow push inland each day (quicker across the Treasure \nCoast). A late day/early evening sea breeze collision both \ntoday/Mon will occur across the eastern peninsula. Storm steering \nwill be light NWRLY today and light WRLY on Mon which will bring \nactivity back toward the coast and across the intracoastal and \nnear shore Atlc waters. Former troughing in the mid-levels moves \naway from the area today, but additional east-west oriented \ntroughing will slide down the peninsula on Mon. Temps at 500 mb \nremain fairly warm at -5.5C to -6.5C. Ample moisture will surge \nback into the area late today and continue across the area for \nMon. Still mainly diurnal PoPs 30-60% today (highest north \nBrevard-Orange County northward) and 70-80% for Mon with mid-level \nenergy enhancing afternoon/evening convection. Primary storm \nimpacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds locally \n35-50 mph, and torrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a \nrelatively short period promoting minor/nuisance flooding in slow-\nmoving storms or repeated rounds. \n\nMax temps slightly above climo today in the L90s (few M90s possible) \nand U80s to L90s for Mon. Humid overnight with mins in the 70s. Peak \nheat indices continue 100-107F ahead of daily storms.\n\nTue-Sat...The east-west oriented trough in the mid-levels across \ncentral FL lingers across the area thru Wed, then gradually weakens \nand lifts northward as nearly zonal flow returns toward late week. \nAt the surface, a weak pressure pattern will remain in place. The \naforementioned weak frontal boundary hovers near north/central to \ncentral FL into mid-week before washing out. The NBM continues to \nkey in on deep moisture surging in proximity of the boundary, \nthus favoring higher than normal PoPs Tue (70-80%), Wed-Thu \n(60-70%), with only a slight dip Fri-Sat (50-60%). Storm impacts \ncontinue to revolve around locally heavy rainfall. The ECSB will \ndevelop each afternoon, and may be able to penetrate further \ninland during much of this period. Storm motion remains fairly \nweak and variable (erratic), but expect some activity to \nperiodically push back toward the coast, esp surrounding stronger \nboundary collisions. Generally near seasonal temps for highs \n(U80s-L90s) with lows in the 70s and conditions humid.",
